<script nonce="{{.Nonce}}">
// ===================== Portal shared helpers =====================
// esc() is the ONE HTML-escape path for the whole portal (P28-0a): every pane
// script that builds markup for innerHTML / a template literal passes any
// server- or catalog-derived string through it, with no exemption by origin.
// It is declared here and nowhere else. portal_partials_test.go (checkPortalEsc)
// pins the declaration line below verbatim, rejects any other declaration or
// assignment of the name in any partial, and requires this partial to be
// served before every script that uses esc.
//
// Why a top-level `const` and not `function esc` (or window.esc):
//  - A top-level const/let in a classic script is a binding in the global
//    Environment Record's [[DeclarativeRecord]], which is shared by every
//    script of the document (ECMA-262, Global Environment Records and
//    GlobalDeclarationInstantiation). A bare `esc` inside any other script's
//    IIFE resolves to it through the global scope, so a pane needs no import.
//  - It is NOT a property of window. `window.esc`, `self.esc = ...`,
//    `window['esc'] = ...` and Object.defineProperty(window, 'esc', ...)
//    neither read nor replace it: bare-name lookup finds the lexical binding
//    first.
//  - `esc = ...` anywhere is a TypeError (assignment to a constant), and a
//    later script declaring the same name (`const|let|var|function esc`) fails
//    at GlobalDeclarationInstantiation with a SyntaxError before any of that
//    script runs. Both are fail-closed: the original esc keeps escaping.
//  - TDZ: this only holds if the const line is the FIRST statement of this
//    script (checkPortalEsc enforces it). A statement before it that throws
//    aborts the script with esc still uninitialised, and every pane then
//    dies with "Cannot access 'esc' before initialization" (a hoisted
//    function declaration would have survived). Put any new helper AFTER it.
//  - A bare global, like PORTAL_TABS in core-router.tmpl; the portal's other
//    shared names are not namespaced either.
//
// Null-safe: null/undefined render as '' (never the text "null"/"undefined").
// Every other value goes through String(), so a number or boolean is escaped
// as its string form.
const esc = (s) => String(s == null ? '' : s).replace(/[&<>"']/g, c => ({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
</script>
